Customize inline answers <p>Inline queries and buttons on inline messages have no chat to send a prompt to, so the guard answers with an article (inline query) or an alert (button). To replace these answers, pass a function that receives the handler context and the channels the user has not joined:</p> <pre><code>def join_first_inline(ctx, channels):
    ...  # answer the inline query yourself

def join_first_callback(ctx, channels):
    ...  # answer a button press on an inline message

@inline_handler("*")
@sponsor_required(on_inline=join_first_inline)
def search(ctx):
    ...

@callback_handler("like:*")
@sponsor_required(on_callback=join_first_callback)
def like(ctx):
    ...
</code></pre> <p>Pass only what you need: an argument you leave out keeps the default answer. <code>@sponsor_required</code> without arguments still works.</p> <p>Note</p> <p>Telegram limits an alert text to 200 characters.</p> Check every update <p>To check every update instead of single handlers, use the guard in a before handler.</p> Use the guard directly <pre><code>guard = SponsorGuard(ctx)
missing = guard.get_not_joined(use_cache=False)   # list of ChannelSponsor
allowed = guard.ensure_joined()                  # True, or sends the prompt
</code></pre>

bot_handlers/help.py<pre><code>from dj_telegram_bot.core.handlers import command_handler

@command_handler("help")
def help_(ctx):
    ctx.bot.send_message(ctx.chat_id, "Send /start to begin.")
</code></pre>

bot_handlers/shop.py<pre><code>from dj_telegram_bot.core.handlers import callback_handler

@callback_handler("buy_*")
def buy(ctx):
    item = ctx.callback_data.removeprefix("buy_")
    ctx.bot.answer_callback_query(ctx.callback_query.id, text=f"Added {item}")
</code></pre> <p>Build the buttons themselves in the admin: Messages &amp; keyboards.</p>

bot_handlers/fallback.py<pre><code>from dj_telegram_bot.core.handlers import message_handler

@message_handler("*", priority=-10)   # tried last
def fallback(ctx):
    ctx.bot.send_message(ctx.chat_id, "Sorry, I didn't understand that.")
</code></pre>

bot_handlers/shop.py<pre><code>from dj_telegram_bot.core.handlers import UpdateHandler, command_handler, callback_handler

class Shop(UpdateHandler):
    @command_handler("shop")
    def open_shop(self):
        self.bot.send_message(self.chat_id, "Welcome to the shop")

    @callback_handler("buy_*")
    def buy(self):
        item = self.callback_data.removeprefix("buy_")
        self.bot.answer_callback_query(self.callback_query.id, text=f"Added {item}")
</code></pre>

Handlers in <code>views.py</code> or another module <p>If your handlers live elsewhere (for example in <code>views.py</code>), import that module yourself so the decorators run. <code>apps.py</code> is a good place:</p> your_app/apps.py<pre><code>from django.apps import AppConfig

class YourAppConfig(AppConfig):
    name = "your_app"

    def ready(self):
        from . import views  # wherever your handlers live
</code></pre>

Member What it is <code>ctx.user</code> the Telegram <code>User</code> who sent the update <code>ctx.user_id</code> its id (<code>0</code> if the update has no user) <code>ctx.user_obj</code> your database user, or <code>None</code> if there is none <code>ctx.has_started_bot</code> <code>True</code> if a database user exists <code>ctx.user_step</code> the current step, or <code>""</code> <code>ctx.set_step(step)</code> save a new step bot_handlers/profile.py<pre><code>@command_handler("profile")
def profile(ctx):
    user = ctx.user_obj
    if user is None:
        return ctx.bot.send_message(ctx.chat_id, "Send /start first.")

    ctx.bot.send_message(ctx.chat_id, f"Your step: {user.step}")
</code></pre> <p>Warning</p> <p><code>user_obj</code> raises <code>ImproperlyConfigured</code> when your model has no <code>user_id</code> and <code>step</code> fields. In that case use <code>ctx.user_id</code>.</p> <p><code>set_step()</code> raises <code>LookupError</code> if there is no database user.</p>
